there are no heights queer people can't  reach literally  hello and welcome to an episode of  historical icons who weren't actually  straight today's icon is sally ride she  was an astronaut physicist tennis player  and the first american woman to go to  space known for her wit and cool  composure she broke gender barrier after  gender barrier and several male  dominated fields and she was gay  up first  sally kristen ride was born in 1951 to  dale and joyce ride dale was a political  science professor and both parents were  presbyterian elders no small feat for a  woman in that era joyce once responded  to a question on how she had raised such  strong ambitious daughters with a laugh  saying in a way you could look at it as  neglect dale and i simply forgot to tell  them that there were things they  couldn't do as a child ryde was  ambitious bright and extremely good at  tennis but gave up her plans to go pro  to focus on school fascinated by lasers  and physics ryan went to several schools  eventually landing at stanford  university in 1973 where she earned a  bachelor's and master's in physics and a  doctorate in astrophysics she also  picked up an english degree you know  just for fun and her last doctorate year  1977 she saw an ad in the school paper  for nasa and out of 8 000 applicants she  was picked by nasa as one of only six  women astronaut candidates to join what  became known as the 35 new guys  dang sally you are a badass  unfortunately sally and her fellow  female trainees had to handle their fair  share of sexism sally answered sex's  press conference questions with humor  and composure returning at one point why  doesn't anybody ask rick those questions  described as a reluctant role model ryde  was often surprised that people were so  curious and impressed with her  accomplishments seemingly just because  she was a woman  saying it may be too bad that our  society isn't further along and that  this is such a big deal  on june 18 1983 she became the first  american woman and the youngest american  ever to go to space the 1983 challenger  mission was six days long during which  she probably did a bunch of cool flips  oh and also deployed two communication  satellites and carried out a variety of  experiments on earth's atmosphere  in 1984 rides served on the second space  mission aboard challenger this time she  had a friendly face on board her  childhood friend katherine sullivan who  became the first american woman to walk  in space these women were literally out  of this world  in january 1986 ryde was on the ground  training for her third space mission  when an unthinkable tragedy occurred the  challenger launched exploded killing  everyone on board  ryde was subsequently appointed to the  presidential commission to investigate  the accident after the catastrophic  event nasa suspended shuttle flights for  more than two years  in 1987 raj decided to explore new  adventures and left nasa she taught  physics at the university of california  and wrote and collaborated on multiple  children's books on space she founded  sally ride science an organization that  encouraged children especially young  girls and minorities to pursue science  math and technology saying simply you  can't be what you can't see  ryde was diagnosed with pancreatic  cancer at age 59 and died at home on  july 23 2012.  now on to the queen  bride was married for five years to  fellow astronaut stephen hawley and she  also privately identified as a gay woman  ryde preferred to focus on her  profession and never made any public  statements about her sexuality when ride  was diagnosed with cancer she and her  life partner of over 27 years tam  o'shaughnessy decided to be more open  about their relationship becoming  certified domestic partners in  california and introducing each other as  my partner it was only in her obituary  written by o'shaughnessy with ride's  blessing that ride officially came out  to the public for the first time the  public learned more about ride's  personal life and her long-term  relationship with tam a children's  science writer professor and former  professional tennis player they became a  couple in 1985 two years after sally's  first historic space mission although  they had first met while playing tennis  when they were 12 and 13. i guess love  does mean something in tennis after all  ry's obituary shocked the nation  remember the fight for legalization of  same-sex marriage was raging at this  time and the national marriage equality  act wouldn't pass for another three  years in america some thought it an  outrage and others celebrated the  discovery of a new lgbtq icon  in spite of her fears about how her  sexuality would be perceived support  provide came out tenfold after she died  sarah mcbride told space.com that ryan  demonstrated to the world that lgbtq  people can be astronauts and that we can  be a part of every single field and  industry and that we can do incredible  things in those jobs and in 2013  president barack obama posthumously gave  ride the presidential medal of freedom  accepted in person by o'shaughnessy  o'shaughnessy said ryde struggled  internally with accepting her sexuality  while working at nasa not wanting to  hurt the organization by probably coming  out she referred to the presentation of  the medal as her and sally's national  coming out saying she was grateful for  the posthumous recognition riot is still  the first and only acknowledged lgbtq  plus astronaut although she wasn't  publicly out during her lifetime ride  always pushed for inclusion she always  advocated for representation in male  dominated careers to both o'shaughnessy  and ride not having role models while  growing up was difficult tam stated that  having role models that are visible in  our society and around the world is  priceless  ride shows the less represented that  they too can reach greatness and follow  their dreams  acknowledgement for ride's achievements  have only grown as the years go by she  will be one of the first women featured  on quarters beginning in 2022 and a  biopic titled ride to be produced and  directed by joey soloway is currently in  development  in conclusion  so there you have it sally ride went all  the way to space but will forever be  remembered for who she was on earth her  legacy opens the door for all of us who  come after her and that's truly stellar  check out another episode of historical  icons here
 

